WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.362
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:09.315
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:15.565
Unit eighteen: Public History. Today you will state general truths about heritage with the present simple.

4
00:00:15.865 --> 00:00:18.401
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:22.437
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:24.528
Public history.

7
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:29.234
Public history means history presented to and with the general public.

8
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.568
Public history happens in museums, films and festivals.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:36.859
Your turn. Say: Public history.

10
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:40.864
Public history.

11
00:00:41.617 --> 00:00:42.653
Museum.

12
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.832
Museum means a building where historical or cultural objects are displayed.

13
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:51.206
The National Museum displays Angkor sculpture.

14
00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:54.132
Your turn. Say: Museum.

15
00:00:56.967 --> 00:00:57.910
Museum.

16
00:00:58.650 --> 00:00:59.814
Heritage site.

17
00:01:00.817 --> 00:01:04.314
Heritage site means a place of historical or cultural importance.

18
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:07.230
Preah Vihear is a World Heritage site.

19
00:01:07.817 --> 00:01:10.771
Your turn. Say: Heritage site.

20
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:15.052
Heritage site.

21
00:01:15.817 --> 00:01:17.224
Interpretation.

22
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:22.248
Interpretation means explaining the meaning of exhibits to visitors.

23
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.813
Good interpretation tells a story, not just facts.

24
00:01:26.417 --> 00:01:29.023
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

25
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:33.204
Interpretation.

26
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.392
Accessibility.

27
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:39.614
Accessibility means ease of access for all people.

28
00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:43.261
Ramps improved accessibility for wheelchair users.

29
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.723
Your turn. Say: Accessibility.

30
00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:51.044
Accessibility.

31
00:01:51.783 --> 00:01:53.330
Community engagement.

32
00:01:54.317 --> 00:01:57.837
Community engagement means involving local people in a project.

33
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.042
Community engagement shaped the exhibition.

34
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:05.312
Your turn. Say: Community engagement.

35
00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:09.795
Community engagement.

36
00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:11.516
Curation.

37
00:02:12.517 --> 00:02:16.095
Curation means selecting and organising items for display.

38
00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:19.124
Curation involves difficult choices.

39
00:02:19.717 --> 00:02:22.032
Your turn. Say: Curation.

40
00:02:24.867 --> 00:02:25.903
Curation.

41
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:27.674
Outreach.

42
00:02:28.683 --> 00:02:31.774
Outreach means programmes that take a service to people.

43
00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:35.184
The museum's outreach visits rural schools.

44
00:02:35.783 --> 00:02:38.459
Your turn. Say: Outreach.

45
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.092
Outreach.

46
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.446
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:52.151
Visal, why does public history matter, if academic historians already write books?

48
00:02:53.250 --> 00:03:02.799
Because most people never read academic books. Museums make history accessible to everyone, and heritage sites connect people to their past.

49
00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:05.813
Does every museum tell the truth?

50
00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:18.741
Not always. Curation involves choices: every exhibition highlights some stories and leaves others out, so good museums explain their interpretation.

51
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.884
How can communities take part?

52
00:03:22.983 --> 00:03:32.369
Local people know things archives don't. When a museum includes them, visitors learn more and communities feel ownership of their heritage.

53
00:03:33.533 --> 00:03:35.266
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:36.117 --> 00:03:39.567
Grammar focus: Present simple for general truths.

55
00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:41.101
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.817 --> 00:03:44.373
Museums make history accessible to everyone.

57
00:03:45.283 --> 00:03:48.688
Every exhibition highlights some stories and leaves others out.

58
00:03:49.583 --> 00:03:52.419
Heritage sites connect people to their past.

59
00:03:53.367 --> 00:04:03.240
Use the present simple for facts, functions and general truths: 'Museums preserve objects.' Remember the -s with singular subjects: 'Curation involves choices'.

60
00:04:03.983 --> 00:04:08.273
Be careful! Don't say: Museums makes history accessible.

61
00:04:10.233 --> 00:04:12.848
Say: Museums make history accessible.

62
00:04:13.148 --> 00:04:14.788
Plural subject: make.

63
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.020
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:20.287
History for Everyone.

65
00:04:20.587 --> 00:04:25.222
Public history refers to the many ways in which the past is presented beyond the university.

66
00:04:26.233 --> 00:04:32.064
Museums, heritage sites, documentaries and anniversaries shape how most citizens understand their history.

67
00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:39.199
Because displays require selection, curation always involves decisions about which stories deserve attention.

68
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:46.460
Increasingly, institutions invite communities to help interpret their own heritage, rather than speaking for them.

69
00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:53.855
Effective public history therefore combines scholarly accuracy with accessibility and genuine participation.

70
00:04:54.967 --> 00:04:59.201
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:02.607
The museum blank free entry on Sundays.

72
00:05:02.907 --> 00:05:06.224
A, offer. B, offers. C, is offer.

73
00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:11.987
The answer is B: offers.

74
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.080
Choose the correct general truth.

75
00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:22.679
A, Heritage sites attracts tourists.. B, Heritage sites attract tourists.. C, Heritage sites are attract tourists..

76
00:05:26.233 --> 00:05:30.044
The answer is B: Heritage sites attract tourists..

77
00:05:31.183 --> 00:05:34.181
Selecting and organising items for display is blank.

78
00:05:34.481 --> 00:05:38.471
A, outreach. B, accessibility. C, curation.

79
00:05:42.033 --> 00:05:43.928
The answer is C: curation.

80
00:05:45.083 --> 00:05:47.652
A place of historical importance is a blank.

81
00:05:47.952 --> 00:05:52.871
A, heritage site. B, community engagement. C, public history.

82
00:05:56.433 --> 00:05:59.199
The answer is A: heritage site.

83
00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:04.683
According to the reading, institutions increasingly invite communities to…

84
00:06:04.983 --> 00:06:09.740
A, pay more. B, help interpret their heritage. C, stay away.

85
00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:16.414
The answer is B: help interpret their heritage.

86
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.612
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:25.609
What museum or heritage site has taught you something meaningful about history?

88
00:06:42.417 --> 00:06:43.894
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:44.194 --> 00:07:19.536
The National Museum of Cambodia taught me something meaningful about the skill of Khmer artists in the Angkor period. Seeing the sculptures closely made me realise how advanced our ancestors' craftsmanship and religious ideas were. The museum also explains how some statues were looted and later returned, which shows that heritage is still connected to present-day justice. Some visitors feel that museums display objects without enough context. Even so, I believe they help young people take pride in their history.

90
00:07:19.836 --> 00:07:21.499
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:22.383 --> 00:07:27.451
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present simple for general truths.

92
00:07:27.751 --> 00:07:32.934
Today's value is public engagement. Share knowledge so that everyone can learn from it.

93
00:07:33.234 --> 00:07:35.921
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:36.221 --> 00:07:39.477
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

